!: kinematic data based on 2003 nuclear masses
!: d-b.u. threshold 4.450 45 MeV
!: 2n,2p  threshold 8.903 54 MeV
!:
!: 2007: up to 40.0 MeV identical with ENDF-VII evaluation by HALE and DROSG
!:
!: 2000-02-20 Bystritskij et al.: 
! .0018   4.3 e-9 barns
! .00206  9.8 e-9
! .00227  2.1 e-8
!: 2000-02-18 Below 0.02 MeV from Abramovich
!: 1991-09-xx: p-T and d-D appear to be low by (1.51 +- 0.35%) at 13.6 and 
!: 12.305 MeV when compared with the reference data of Jarmie, whereas d-T 
!: between 10 to 14 MeV appears to be high by (1.49 +- 0.46)% 
!: when compared with the scales of Jarmie, Ivanovich and Stewart.
!: 1989-05-18 MODIFIED BELOW 0.25 MeV (scale +1%, anisotropy) according to      
!: Brown and Jarmie, to be published in Phys.Rev. C
!: Data from 0.4 to 3.0 MeV straight from Liskien&Paulsen
!: High energy end: semilog. fit through 85 MeV data point
!: 65LY: at 58.4 MeV/0deg (17.97(corr.)+-2.9) mb/sr
!:
!: ATTENTION: the code assumes that a given coefficient is zero if at any 
!: higher energy there are no nonzero coefficients of the same or a higher 
!: order!!!
!: If single angle data (e.g. 0 deg, 180 deg) are added to Legendre tables the
!: following steps must be taken:
!1: Add min. energy(EMINL) and maximum energy(EMAXL) of Legendre values in 
!: 1st line
!2: make first added energy value EMAXL+0.1 keV
!3: give AT LEAST 5 additional energy values

!: 19980813: very low energy estimate at 0.00044 MeV: < 2.10^-10 b, 
